  "description_html": "Size of genera in the extinct bird family Confuciusornithidae, compared to a human (1.75 meter tall). <b>A.</b> <i>Changchengornis</i>. Based on the holotype.<sup id=\"cite_ref-1\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-1\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">[</span>1<span class=\"cite-bracket\">]</span></a></sup> <b>B.</b> <i>Confuciusornis</i>. Based on several specimens of about the same size.<sup id=\"cite_ref-2\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-2\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">[</span>2<span class=\"cite-bracket\">]</span></a></sup> <b>C.</b> <i>Eoconfuciusornis</i>. Based on the holotype IVPP V11977.<sup id=\"cite_ref-3\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-3\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">[</span>3<span class=\"cite-bracket\">]</span></a></sup>\n<div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading4\"><h4 id=\"References\">References</h4></div>\n<ol class=\"references\">\n<li id=\"cite_note-1\"><span class=\"mw-cite-backlink\"><a href=\"#cite_ref-1\">↑</a></span> <span class=\"reference-text\">Chiappe L.M. <i>et.al.</i> (1999), <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://dinosaurs.nhm.org/staff/pdf/1999Qiang_et_al.pdf\">\"A new Late Mesozoic Confuciusornithid Bird from China\"</a>, <i>Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology</i> <b>19</b>(1): p. 1-7.</span>\n</li>\n<li id=\"cite_note-2\"><span class=\"mw-cite-backlink\"><a href=\"#cite_ref-2\">↑</a></span> <span class=\"reference-text\">Norell M.A. <i>et.al.</i> (1999), <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://dinosaurs.nhm.org/staff/pdf/1999Chiappe_et_al.pdf\">\"Anatomy and systematics of the Confuciusornithidae (Theropoda: Aves) from the late Mesozoic of northeastern China\"</a>, <i>Bulletin of the American Museum of Natural History</i> <i>242</i>(10).</span>\n</li>\n<li id=\"cite_note-3\"><span class=\"mw-cite-backlink\"><a href=\"#cite_ref-3\">↑</a></span> <span class=\"reference-text\">Benton M.J. <i>et.al.</i> (2008), <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" dat